From: CLS2TDB9@ibm3090.bham.ac.uk
Date: Wed, 07 Dec 94 20:00:12 GMT
Subject: [#94-12-288] BASICS: Connectionism

The Extropian list is by its very nature 'interdisciplinary' - that is what
makes it the lively and immensely fruitful discussion space that it is.
However, I'm interested in what everybody thinks about "Connectionism" (the
term which is included in a glossary in _Extropy_) as an increasingly, if not
already, crucial explanatory framework for these sorts of ideas.
     Certainly in Cultural Studies - my 'discipline' - the theoretical
apparatus which Connectionism provides is incredibly important. It is no longer
merely a tool in the theorists repertoire alongside ethnography, discourse
analysis etc., but more and more it is the _only_ way to begin to discuss these
areas. What does anybody else think? How useful is it to conceptualise flows
of retrievable 'information' which occasionally crystalise into particular sets
of 'knowledges' and then become 'disciplines'? How discrete are these subject
boundaries? How profitable is it to draw on different theoretical/practical
areas, and if we do this, can we do it without undervaluing each one? Please
feel free to take me up on these things - at the moment they're just
relatively rough late-evening thoughts, but I think it's very important for us
to be clear about the sorts of apparatuses for explanation that might be useful
to us.
     I think the sort of connectionist approach fostered by this list and the
people on it allows us to think *around* the issues with a comforting open-
mindedness; certainly the diversity of interests collated here makes for a
really rich and rewarding debate. Comments please :-)

Tim Burdsey      cls2tdb9@bham.ac.uk                      Up, Up and AWAY!

