		Meteorite Crater Under Chesapeake Bay?
		--------------------------------------

        From Astronomy, January, 1995, Vol. 23, No. 11, Pg 24

    About 35 million years ago a large meteorite blasted a crater at least 
    85 km across at the mouth of what is now Chesapeake Bay, says C. Wylie
    Poag of the U.S. Geological Survey in Woods Hole, Massachusetts.  Poag
    and fellow USGS scientists identified what they believe to be a buried
    impact crater using seismic reflection techniques to image rock layers
    under Chesapeake Bay.  They also studied jumbled sediments found on
    land in Virginia and the Delmarva peninsula; their report was published
    in the August 1994 issue of GEOLOGY. 

    The seismic profiles show disturbed rock layers below the present bay 
    floor that resemble a cross section of an impact crater.  The land
    outcrops contain broken rocks and jumbled sediments that the team
    interprets as coming from tsunamis (commonly referred to as tidal
    waves). At the time of impact, the site was about 100 km offshore. 

    The geologists think the crater could also be the source for a field of 
    tektites that stretches from Barbados to New Jersey to Texas.  Tektites
    are glassy rocks formed when impact-melted rock is flung high into
    space, chills quickly, and falls again to Earth. 

    Poag and his colleagues are now searching through deposits looking for 
    telltale signs of high-velocity impact shocks.  These are considered by
    geologists to be diagnostic of meteorite impacts. 

