From: CurtAdams@aol.com
Date: Wed, 7 Dec 1994 15:57:36 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-291] Changing Viewed Spectra

Da'Claw wrote:

>        What if we were able to change this chemical, or alter rhodopsin so
that
>it reacted to light from a higher/lower wavelength?I postulate that we would
>'see' light in the infrared/ultraviolet spectrum, but this might cause
>difficulties at the level of the optic cortex-once again, a period of
retraining
>might follow before the human could make full use of his new range of
vision.

There are many rhodopsins - every animal has a different set.  Mammal
rhodopsins are (I believe) all related to the 3 we have.  Some birds,
however, have four families, and come insects have rhodopsins tuned to
significantly higher frequencies than our blue rhodopsin.  I don't know if
any creatures have reds significantly lower than ours.

Engineering a human zygote to replace one of the human rhodopsins with any of
these animal rhodopsins would be within the reach of current technology,
although most researchers would consider it unethical and I suspect it would
be illegal in most places.  Such a human would grow up with a different
perception of colors than current humans, and his/her range of color
perception could be expanded.  

There is already some variation in color perception among human.  Like most
genes, human rhodopsin genes all have multiple alleles, with (sometimes)
slightly differing peak absorption frequencies.  The brain adjudicates color
by comparing responses from the various rhodopsins.  Different peak
absorption frequencies cause different ratios for the same input frequency,
so a different perceived color.

When I was a kid, my classmates had fierce arguements over whether sodium
vapor lamps were orange or pink (I see orange, as did a majority of my
classmates).  Nobody thought that maybe our eyes differed.  Now I wonder
whether there was a difference in our rhodopsins.

Seeing into UV would require replacing the lens as well.  Human lenses are
opaque to UV.  Some cataract patients report seeing new colors after a
cataract operation as the blue rhodopsin does have some sensitivity to low UV
frequencies.  The artifical lenses sometime permit in UV frequencies blocked
by human lenses.  I would worry about the effects of UV on the retina as
there is no current evolutionary need for our retinal cells to be able to
resist UV.

Giving a human a more sophisticated color system - like the bird 4-color
system rather than the primate 3-color - would be much more difficult.  The
optic nerve and optic cortex are only geared up to carry and analyze three
different color signals.  Adding a fourth would require advances in our
knowledge on development biology as we do not yet know much about the genetic
controls for that.  I would suspect that changes would require multiple
genetic changes (unlike the single change for the rhodopsin switch).

As usual, changing a grown human in such a fundamental way is harder than
changing an embryo.  Rhodopsins are modified proteins and not very amenable
to alterations via diet.  Gene swapping in all your retinal cells would be
tough, although the relative accessability of retinal cells makes it easier
than usual.  Adding more channels to the optic nerve would be VERY tough -
even if modified, the existing developmental genes have done their thing and
mostly would not operate properly in an adult.  One could not use them for
help and would probably have to put in a whole new development system.

The change might be really cool, though.  Remember the brain adjudicates
color by response ratios?  There are three rhodopsins but only a
one-dimensional range of frequencies.  In frequencies where all three
rhodopsins operate (about 475-525 nm - greenish colors), changing one would
create new ratios one had never experienced before.  Hence, new colors.
 WARNING - one would also LOSE existing colors in those frequencies, at least
until one switched the rhodopsin back.

