Subject: [#94-12-295] ASTRO: My problem with astrology

Vince (vincek@intergalact.com) writes:

> Thus, Jupiter exerts a force on me just over half the weight of a raindrop.
> 
> So, which should I check first: my horoscope, or the weather report?
> 
> --Vince
> 

To kind of switch sides on this issue, I would point out that Jupiter
exerts a periodic force, whereas a raindrop (or rather rainfall) exerts
a chaotic force.  So you may have to consider more than order of magnitude
in making such comparisons.  

Thanks also to the person (sorry; I've lost the message; maybe 
Nancy L.?) who pointed out that other periodicities (seasonal changes,
rising/setting of the sun) have not prevented the emergence and
growth of life.  I thought about this yesterday after I'd posted my
message, and I knew that someone would call me on it!

I had thought more along the lines of periodic *characteristics* 
reducing a species' chance of survival, because, e.g., some predator 
species could evolve to exploit the periodicity.  I have heard this 
theory as an explanation of species that reproduce in a prime number of
years, like the 17-year locust.

Whether or not such periodicity would prove catastrophic enough to
select against in general, remains an open question.

